LEVEL 4 → 5 — IDENTIFYING THE HUMAN-READABLE FILE


WHAT THIS LEVEL IS ABOUT : 

The inhere directory contains ten files (-file00 through -file09), only one of which contains human-readable text. The rest are binary. This level introduces the file command, which identifies file content based on magic bytes rather than file extensions.


APPROACH : 

My initial approach was manual — opening each file one by one with cat ./-file00, cat ./-file01, and so on. Most returned garbled binary output. The efficient solution was to run file on all files at once using shell globbing:
file ./*
This immediately revealed that -file07 was the only one identified as ASCII text. I then read it:
cat ./-file07

Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]


KEY TAKEAWAY :

Never rely on file extensions to determine file type — they can be changed or absent. The file command reads magic bytes to determine a file's true format. This is fundamental in malware analysis and digital forensics.
